      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the properties of modular arithmetic. We are given that when four positive integers are divided by \\(11\\), the remainders are \\(2\\), \\(4\\), \\(6\\), and \\(8\\). This means we can express these integers as:\n\\[\na = 11k + 2, \\quad b = 11m + 4, \\quad c = 11n + 6, \\quad d = 11o + 8\n\\]\nwhere \\(k, m, n, o\\) are integers. We need to find the remainder when the sum of these integers is divided by \\(11\\). First, we calculate the sum of the integers:\n\\[\na + b + c + d = (11k + 2) + (11m + 4) + (11n + 6) + (11o + 8) = 11(k + m + n + o) + 20\n\\]\nNext, we need to determine the remainder when \\(11(k + m + n + o) + 20\\) is divided by \\(11\\). Since \\(11(k + m + n + o)\\) is a multiple of \\(11\\), it gives a remainder of \\(0\\) when divided by \\(11\\). Therefore, we only need to find the remainder when \\(20\\) is divided by \\(11\\):\n\\[\n20 \\div 11 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 9\n\\]\nThus, the remainder when \\(20\\) is divided by \\(11\\) is \\(9\\). Therefore, the remainder when the sum of the four integers is divided by \\(11\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
